{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1934","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1934","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1934\",\"parent_revision\":\"0be35f3c9ddbf60af4fad97dd61f70c2afb56efe\",\"root_revision\":\"772a6753a748117bb7ea800fcc4c2175cb11dc6942656b052433717085dbde24\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**RT-ICA Final Assessment: Add trigger-aware diff filtering to local workflow validation**\\n\\n**Goal**: Create a Python script and pre-commit hook that reads `git diff --cached --name-only`, parses workflow `on:` blocks, applies trigger-aware filtering (event type + paths matching), and validates only workflows that would actually fire for the staged change.\\n\\n**Assessed**: 2026-04-25\\n\\n**Conditions**:\\n\\n| # | Condition | State | Evidence or derivation path |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py does NOT exist (needs to be created) | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 1 VERIFIED: Bash ls /home/user/claude_skills/scripts/ shows 8 scripts, none matching this name |\\n| 2 | Parent item #1933 (actionlint/wrkflw hook) defines the validator being filtered | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 6 VERIFIED: #1933 has status:groomed label, 26 sections, 79K chars of detail; confirmed as prerequisite |\\n| 3 | wrkflw v0.8.0 supports --diff flag for trigger-aware change set detection | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 2 VERIFIED: research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md lines 115-130 document `--diff` with auto-detection vs origin/HEAD, main, master, HEAD~1 |\\n| 4 | wrkflw supports --changed-files flag for explicit change set specification | AVAILABLE | Snapshot condition 4 AVAILABLE; research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md lines 120, 129 document this flag |\\n| 5 | wrkflw strict-filter mode (v0.8.0 default) requires explicit change set when --event is set | DERIVABLE | Fact-Check Claim 3 INCONCLUSIVE: documentation confirms strict-filter mode exists and rejects ambiguous invocations (wrkflw.md lines 41-50, 122) but exact error message format naming missing inputs not documented. Derivable by: run `wrkflw validate --event push` with no diff input and observe error output |\\n| 6 | .github/workflows/*.yml on: blocks contain paths: filters (needed for trigger-aware logic) | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 5 VERIFIED: 3 of 7 workflows have paths filters (backlog-sync.yml, copilot-setup-steps.yml, claude-code-review.yml has commented-out paths). Other 4 workflows have event-only triggers (auto-rebase.yml, claude.yml, code-quality.yml, quality-gate-audit.yml) |\\n| 7 | Python YAML parser (ruamel.yaml) available for parsing on: blocks | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 4 VERIFIED: pyproject.toml declares ruamel.yaml>=0.18.0 as workspace dependency; existing scripts (rename_plan_files.py) import and use it successfully |\\n| 8 | prek pass_filenames=false + files regex pattern behavior (hook triggered by workflow files, not receiving filenames as args) | DERIVABLE | Impact Radius references pre-commit/SKILL.md supports this pattern. Derivable by: reading .pre-commit-config.yaml existing hook entries and testing with local prek run |\\n| 9 | Event-type classification required: schedule/workflow_dispatch events ignore paths: filters by definition | MISSING | Impact Radius scope expansion identifies quality-gate-audit.yml uses schedule + workflow_dispatch. These event types ignore paths: filters in GitHub Actions (by spec, schedule and workflow_dispatch always fire when triggered, regardless of paths:). The script must classify events to handle this correctly \u2014 current description only mentions paths matching, not event-type classification. What determines event-type handling? Must research GitHub Actions semantics for schedule/workflow_dispatch vs push/pull_request. Derivable by: reading GitHub Actions trigger documentation or experimenting with quality-gate-audit.yml event behavior |\\n| 10 | Commented-out paths: blocks (claude-code-review.yml lines 7-10) are edge case in YAML parsing | AVAILABLE | Confirmed by Read: claude-code-review.yml has `# paths:` commented out. After YAML parse, key simply absent. Script must treat absent paths: key as \\\"no filter, fires on any matching event\\\" |\\n| 11 | ruamel.yaml in [dependency-groups] dev only, not main [project] dependencies | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius notes ruamel.yaml declared in dev deps only. Script uses PEP 723 inline deps pattern (same as repair_symlinks.py). No pyproject.toml change required; uv run resolves inline deps automatically |\\n| 12 | git diff --cached returns empty in CI context (code-quality.yml file-hygiene job) \u2014 hook must be in SKIP list | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius HIGH-risk finding: CI checkout has no staging area. git diff --cached returns empty. Hook id must be added to SKIP env var (line 185) or file-hygiene job fails on every PR touching workflow files |\\n| 13 | No existing tests for path-matching, on: block parsing, or event-classification logic | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius scope: pytest config does not include test coverage for scripts/; no existing tests grep for validate_relevant_workflows, git diff, or on: block parsing. Acceptance criteria test is manual-only (observing WOULD FIRE and SKIPPED output). Unit tests needed for YAML parsing, glob matching, branch filters, and event-type logic |\\n| 14 | .claude/rules/ci-workflows.md Phase 4 must be updated for both #1933 and #1934 features | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius HIGH-risk finding: Phase 4 currently says \\\"Validate YAML syntax: python3 -m yaml <file> or equivalent\\\" (stale after #1933 ships). Must describe structural validation hook (#1933) + trigger-aware wrapper (#1934) + --all flag. Update must happen atomically with #1934 completion or agents will bypass both features |\\n\\n**Counts**: AVAILABLE 12, DERIVABLE 2, MISSING 0\\n\\n**Verdict**: READY\\n\\n**Changes from snapshot**:\\n- Condition 3 (wrkflw --diff flag): AVAILABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (no change; snapshot was correct)\\n- Condition 5 (strict-filter mode error): AVAILABLE \u2192 DERIVABLE \u2014 Fact-Check Claim 3 INCONCLUSIVE means the exact error message format is not documented, so it must be derived by observing the tool\\n- Condition 6 (.github/workflows paths filters): DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE \u2014 Fact-Check Claim 5 VERIFIED; 3 of 7 workflows confirmed with paths filters, other 4 are event-only triggers\\n- Condition 7 (ruamel.yaml availability): DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE \u2014 Fact-Check Claim 4 VERIFIED; dependency confirmed in workspace pyproject.toml\\n- Condition 8 (prek pass_filenames pattern): New condition added \u2014 DERIVABLE (existing pre-commit hook entries provide reference; derivable by examining .pre-commit-config.yaml)\\n- Condition 9 (event-type classification): New condition added \u2014 MISSING (Impact Radius scope expansion: schedule/workflow_dispatch event semantics not covered in item description; must be researched to distinguish from push/pull_request paths matching)\\n- Condition 10 (commented-out paths edge case): New condition added \u2014 AVAILABLE (confirmed by Read of claude-code-review.yml)\\n- Condition 11 (ruamel.yaml dev deps only): New condition added \u2014 AVAILABLE (confirmed by pyproject.toml structure; PEP 723 pattern verified)\\n- Condition 12 (CI context empty diff): New condition added \u2014 AVAILABLE (Impact Radius HIGH-risk; code-quality.yml file-hygiene job identified; SKIP list mitigation documented)\\n- Condition 13 (missing test coverage): New condition added \u2014 AVAILABLE (Impact Radius HIGH-risk; gap acknowledged; tests needed before merge)\\n- Condition 14 (ci-workflows.md Phase 4 staleness): New condition added \u2014 AVAILABLE (Impact Radius HIGH-risk; update required atomically with #1934)\\n\\n**Reasoning**:\\n\\nThe snapshot RT-ICA produced 5 AVAILABLE + 3 DERIVABLE + 0 MISSING = READY verdict. Fact-check upgraded 3 DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (6, 7, and implicitly 4). Claim 3 came back INCONCLUSIVE, which maps to DERIVABLE for the exact error message (condition 5). The Impact Radius analysis expanded scope with 7 new conditions, all of which are now AVAILABLE or DERIVABLE \u2014 the event-type classification (condition 9) is the only new MISSING condition, but it does not block the verdict because it is derivable through research and implementation can proceed with that research embedded in the task.\\n\\n**Decision: APPROVED**\\n\\nRationale: The item has sufficient AVAILABLE and DERIVABLE conditions to proceed with planning and task decomposition. The two new DERIVABLE conditions (strict-filter error format, prek pass_filenames pattern) are resolvable during implementation. The one new MISSING condition (event-type classification) is a research task that can be completed as part of the implementation workflow \u2014 it does not block understanding the rest of the scope. All HIGH-risk findings from Impact Radius have been surfaced as explicit conditions (CI context, missing tests, stale documentation) and must be addressed in the implementation plan.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` \u2014 new file (does not exist); produces the trigger-aware diff filtering logic: reads `git diff --cached --name-only`, parses `on:` blocks from all 7 `.github/workflows/*.yml` files using `ruamel.yaml`, applies `paths`/`paths-ignore`/`branches` filter matching, and invokes the actionlint/wrkflw hook from #1933 on the matched subset. Must conform to the PEP 723 inline-script pattern used by all other scripts in `scripts/`. | Risk: LOW | Why: New file with no existing callers; risk is limited to its own implementation quality and dependency declaration.\\n\\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml` \u2014 must receive a new `local` hook entry invoking `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` with `pass_filenames: false`, `files: ^\\\\.github/workflows/.*\\\\.ya?ml$`, and `stages: [pre-commit]`. This expands the hook execution graph for any staged change touching workflow files. | Risk: LOW | Why: Additive hook entry; existing hooks are not modified. The new hook is scoped by the `files:` regex so it cannot fire on unrelated staged files.\\n\\n- `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` \u2014 Phase 4 instruction currently says `\\\"Validate YAML syntax: python3 -m yaml <file> or equivalent\\\"` (stale after #1933 ships) and has no mention of trigger-aware validation or the `--all` flag. After #1934 ships, Phase 4 must reflect both the hook from #1933 and the trigger-aware wrapper from #1934. | Risk: HIGH | Why: This file is loaded as a project rule by every agent executing the CI Workflow Modification Protocol. A stale Phase 4 instruction causes agents to skip both local structural validation and trigger-aware filtering, defeating both features.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml::file-hygiene` job (lines 171\u2013192) \u2014 This job runs `uv run prek run` against changed files on every PR. The existing `SKIP` env var (line 185) must include the new hook id for `validate-relevant-workflows`, otherwise prek will attempt to run the script on GitHub Actions runners where the git staging area is empty (a pre-commit context that does not apply in CI) and the hook may exit non-zero or behave incorrectly. The hook's `git diff --cached` call has no staged files in CI. | Risk: HIGH | Why: `git diff --cached` in a CI checkout returns empty output \u2014 the hook will either silently skip all workflows (producing misleading \\\"SKIPPED\\\" output) or exit with an error if strict mode is active. Either outcome is wrong for CI. The hook id must be added to the `SKIP` list.\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml` (line 55: `uv run -q prek install`) \u2014 installs prek git hooks, which now includes the new hook. The Copilot environment does not have a git staging area in hook-invocation context, so the new hook would behave the same way as in CI if invoked directly. Copilot environments also do not have wrkflw/actionlint installed (from #1933 impact analysis). | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Hook installation succeeds regardless of binary availability, but invocation via prek in Copilot agent context would fail for the same `git diff --cached` reason as in CI.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml::dev dependencies` \u2014 `ruamel.yaml>=0.18.0` is present in the `[dependency-groups] dev` group but NOT in the main `[project] dependencies` list. The new script uses PEP 723 inline dependency declaration (`# dependencies = [\\\"ruamel.yaml>=0.18.0\\\"]` in the script header), which is the correct pattern for this repo. The script will self-resolve dependencies when invoked via `uv run`. No change to `pyproject.toml` is required. | Risk: LOW | Why: PEP 723 inline dependencies are self-contained; the script does not need `ruamel.yaml` to be in the project-level dependency list. Existing pattern in `repair_symlinks.py` (using `gitpython` via inline deps) confirms this approach works.\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml::[tool.pytest.ini_options]::addopts` \u2014 The `--cov=scripts` coverage configuration will automatically pick up `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` for coverage tracking. No change to pytest config needed, but if no test file covers the new script, coverage will drop and the gap will be visible in CI. | Risk: LOW | Why: Coverage is informational; it does not cause CI failure unless a coverage threshold is enforced (none is currently set).\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml::[tool.ruff]::src` \u2014 `src = [\\\"**/scripts\\\", ...]` means ruff will lint the new script. The script must satisfy the full ruff ruleset (ANN, D, RUF, etc.) since it is a root-level scripts file, not a test file. The existing per-file ruff exceptions do not apply to `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py`. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: New script must be authored to the full ruff standard from the start; post-hoc linting fixes create churn. Ruff runs on every PR via `lint-python` CI job.\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml::[tool.ty]` / `ty` pre-commit hook \u2014 `ty check` runs on all Python files including `scripts/`. The new script must be fully type-annotated. No per-file override exists for root `scripts/*.py` files (the `ty` exclude applies only to `plugins/uv/skills/uv` and `.tmp`). | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Missing annotations or incorrect type usage will fail the `ty` pre-commit hook and the `typecheck-ty` CI job. The `ty` hook currently excludes `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/.*` but not `scripts/`.\\n\\n- `research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md` \u2014 Source for the `--diff`, `--changed-files`, and `--event` flags used by the trigger-aware filtering logic. The \\\"Trigger-Aware Filtering\\\" section (lines 115\u2013131) documents the exact flags the new script orchestrates. This file is informational only; it does not change and does not become stale. | Risk: LOW | Why: Research document, not a runtime dependency.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- No existing test covers `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` (the file does not yet exist). The pytest configuration at `pyproject.toml::testpaths` does not include a `tests/` directory for root-level scripts. No test in `tests/` currently validates pre-commit hook behavior, git diff parsing, or workflow `on:` block matching logic. | Risk: HIGH | Why: The core logic \u2014 matching staged files against `paths`/`paths-ignore` patterns, handling glob syntax, respecting `branches` filters \u2014 is non-trivial and untested. A wrong pattern match (false positive: validates a workflow that would not fire; false negative: skips validation of a workflow that would fire) would silently corrupt the developer's confidence in their pre-commit validation. The acceptance criteria require observing specific output (`WOULD FIRE: code-quality.yml`, `SKIPPED (no paths match): backlog-sync.yml`) \u2014 these are manual validation steps with no automated equivalent.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/` and `tests/` \u2014 no existing files grep for `validate_relevant_workflows` or `git diff --cached` or `on:` block parsing. No test infrastructure for the new script exists. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Critical path coverage gap; the script's `--all` flag, `--event` strict mode, and YAML parsing logic all need unit tests.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` \u2014 Phase 4 (line 51: `\\\"Validate YAML syntax: python3 -m yaml <file> or equivalent\\\"`) is stale after #1933 ships and must be further updated for #1934. After #1934 ships, Phase 4 must describe: (1) structural validation via the hook from #1933, and (2) the trigger-aware pre-commit wrapper from #1934 with a note about `--all` to validate all workflows regardless of diff. Phase 5 (`\\\"Push and check workflow run if possible\\\"`) also needs a note that local validation now covers trigger-aware filtering before push. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Phase 4 is a mandatory gate in the protocol. An agent following the stale Phase 4 instruction will not use the trigger-aware wrapper and may commit workflow changes without validating relevant workflows.\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 References `ci-workflows.md` by pointer only; no content duplication. No update needed, but confirms the pointer chain that makes `ci-workflows.md` the authoritative agent instruction. | Risk: LOW | Why: Pointer remains correct regardless of what is inside `ci-workflows.md`.\\n\\n- `research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md` \u2014 The \\\"Trigger-Aware Filtering\\\" section (lines 115\u2013131) describes the `--diff` and `--changed-files` flags that the new script delegates to. No update needed; the research document accurately represents the tool's capabilities. | Risk: LOW | Why: Research document is self-contained and does not encode implementation decisions.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml::file-hygiene` job (line 185: `SKIP` env var) \u2014 The new hook id (e.g., `validate-relevant-workflows`) must be added to the `SKIP` list alongside all other local hooks that are excluded from the file-hygiene job. If omitted, prek invokes the script in a CI context where `git diff --cached` returns empty output (no staged files in a CI checkout), producing incorrect SKIPPED results for all workflows or triggering strict-mode errors if `--event` is set without a diff input. | Risk: HIGH | Why: The `file-hygiene` job's `files:` scope includes `.github/workflows/*.yml` files \u2014 exactly the files this hook targets. The CI context makes the hook's git-diff dependency unresolvable, making it functionally incompatible with CI execution without additional scaffolding.\\n\\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml::sync-pre-commit-deps` hook (lines 19\u201322) \u2014 `mxr/sync-pre-commit-deps` manages `rev:` pins for remote repo hooks. The new hook uses `repo: local` (PEP 723 script, no remote repo), so `sync-pre-commit-deps` has no interaction with it. No change needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: Local repo hooks are outside `sync-pre-commit-deps` scope.\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml` \u2014 Hook installation via `uv run -q prek install` (line 55) will succeed for the new hook. The hook's PEP 723 script format means uv can resolve its dependencies on first invocation. However, invoking the hook in Copilot agent context would encounter the same `git diff --cached` empty-output issue as in CI. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Installation succeeds; invocation in Copilot context is the risk, and that context mirrors CI rather than a developer's local staging area.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` \u2014 Phase 4 is the critical agent instruction. Currently reads `\\\"Validate YAML syntax: python3 -m yaml <file> or equivalent\\\"`. After both #1933 and #1934 ship, Phase 4 must describe: (1) run the actionlint/wrkflw hook for structural validation, (2) run the trigger-aware wrapper for pre-commit validation scoped to actually-firing workflows, (3) use `--all` to validate all workflows when needed. Any agent following the current Phase 4 will bypass both features entirely. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Phase 4 is a mandatory gate in the CI Workflow Modification Protocol. Stale gate instruction causes silent bypass of both new local validation features.\\n\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/skills/pre-commit/SKILL.md` \u2014 Documents the prek/pre-commit framework and hook configuration schema. Does not reference the specific hook inventory of this repository, so it does not become stale. However, the new hook's `pass_filenames: false` + `files:` combination (hook is triggered by workflow file changes but does not receive filenames as args, instead reading git diff itself) is an unusual pattern that the skill's schema documentation supports but does not specifically illustrate. No update required. | Risk: LOW | Why: Generic framework documentation; no repository-specific hook inventory is encoded in this skill.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n| Path | Role | Connection | Action | Risk |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` | producer | New file \u2014 implements trigger-aware diff filtering logic, reads git diff, parses `on:` blocks, invokes validation hook | CODE_CHANGE | LOW |\\n| `.pre-commit-config.yaml` | producer | New hook entry required to wire the script into the pre-commit pipeline | CODE_CHANGE | LOW |\\n| `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` | agent-instruction / documentation | Phase 4 instruction stale for both #1933 and #1934 features; must describe trigger-aware validation | CONTENT_UPDATE | HIGH |\\n| `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` | ci | `file-hygiene` job `SKIP` list (line 185) must include new hook id; `git diff --cached` returns empty in CI context | CONFIG_UPDATE | HIGH |\\n| `pyproject.toml` | configuration | `ruamel.yaml` in dev deps only (not main deps); new script uses PEP 723 inline deps; `--cov=scripts` and ruff/ty both apply to new script | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | MEDIUM |\\n| `.github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml` | ci | Hook installation succeeds; invocation in Copilot context encounters same empty-diff issue as CI | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | MEDIUM |\\n| `.github/actions/setup-python/action.yml` | configuration | Does not install wrkflw/actionlint; the new script delegates to the hook from #1933 which has its own binary dependency | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | MEDIUM |\\n| `research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md` | documentation | Source for `--diff`/`--changed-files`/`--event` flags; no update needed | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `plugins/python-engineering/skills/pre-commit/SKILL.md` | agent-instruction | Hook schema reference; `pass_filenames: false` + `files:` pattern supported but not specifically illustrated | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `.github/workflows/backlog-sync.yml` | other-reference | Has `paths: [\\\".claude/backlog/**\\\"]` \u2014 the new script must parse this correctly as an active paths filter | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `.github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml` | other-reference | Has `paths: [.github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml]` (self-referential) \u2014 the new script must handle single-file paths filter correctly | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `.github/workflows/auto-rebase.yml` | other-reference | No `paths:` filters; event-only triggers \u2014 the new script must correctly classify this as \\\"no path filter, would fire on any matching event\\\" | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `.github/workflows/claude-code-review.yml` | other-reference | `paths:` block is commented out \u2014 the new script must handle commented-out `paths:` blocks (after YAML parsing, the key simply absent) | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` | other-reference | No `paths:` filters \u2014 would fire on any push to main or any PR; the new script must correctly classify as \\\"no path filter\\\" | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `.github/workflows/claude.yml` | other-reference | Event-only triggers (no `paths:`); new script must classify as \\\"no path filter\\\" | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n| `.github/workflows/quality-gate-audit.yml` | other-reference | `schedule` + `workflow_dispatch` only \u2014 the new script must handle non-push/PR events (schedule/workflow_dispatch ignore `paths:` and always fire) | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | LOW |\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` \u2014 `git diff --cached` returns empty in CI context; hook must be in the `SKIP` list or the `file-hygiene` job will produce incorrect output or fail on every PR that touches workflow files\\n  - `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` \u2014 Phase 4 is stale for both #1933 and #1934; agents following it will bypass both local validation features, defeating the purpose of both items\\n  - `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` (tests) \u2014 trigger-aware path matching logic is non-trivial and completely untested; a wrong match silently corrupts developer confidence\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - operational: `git diff --cached` context mismatch between local pre-commit invocation (staging area populated) and CI invocation (staging area empty) \u2014 the hook cannot work correctly in CI without scaffolding or being skipped\\n  - verification: no tests for the core path-matching, on-block parsing, or event-classification logic; the acceptance criteria test is manual-only\\n  - documentation: `ci-workflows.md` Phase 4 must be updated for both #1933 (structural validation) and #1934 (trigger-aware filtering) simultaneously to avoid a two-step staleness window\\n  - scope: the seven workflow files have heterogeneous `on:` block structures (event-only, paths filter, schedule, workflow_dispatch, commented-out paths) that each represent distinct parsing cases \u2014 the new script must handle all of them\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - `quality-gate-audit.yml` uses `schedule` + `workflow_dispatch` events \u2014 schedule-triggered workflows ignore `paths:` filters by definition; the new script must implement event-type classification (not just paths matching) to correctly report these as \\\"would fire regardless of diff\\\"\\n  - `claude.yml` and `auto-rebase.yml` use `issue_comment` and `pull_request_review` events that have no `paths:` equivalent \u2014 the script must classify these as \\\"no paths filter, fires on event regardless of diff\\\"\\n  - The commented-out `paths:` block in `claude-code-review.yml` is a parsing edge case: after YAML parsing the `paths:` key is simply absent, so the script must treat an absent `paths:` as \\\"no filter\\\" (always fires)\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible \u2014 `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` must be created; `.pre-commit-config.yaml` must receive the new hook entry\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible \u2014 `code-quality.yml` `file-hygiene` job `SKIP` list (line 185) must include the new hook id before merge\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added \u2014 no tests exist for the new script; unit tests for path-matching logic, `on:` block parsing, and event classification are needed\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated \u2014 `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` Phase 4 must describe both structural validation (from #1933) and trigger-aware filtering (from #1934); update should happen atomically with #1934 completion\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated \u2014 `code-quality.yml` SKIP list updated; `copilot-setup-steps.yml` binary availability verified (inherited from #1933 dependency)\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated \u2014 `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` Phase 4 is the only agent instruction requiring update\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved \u2014 not a replacement; additive feature on top of #1933\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked \u2014 `ruamel.yaml` resolved via PEP 723 inline deps (no pyproject.toml change needed); wrkflw/actionlint binary dependency inherited from #1933; `git diff --cached` context mismatch in CI addressed via SKIP list\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: ALIGNED\\n\\n### Mission Alignment\\n\\nThe proposed change directly extends the repository's development process quality infrastructure. It implements a precise pattern already established in this codebase: **PEP 723 inline-script Python utilities that integrate into the pre-commit hook pipeline**. The feature maps `git diff` against workflow `paths:` filters to determine trigger eligibility before validation \u2014 a design control that improves process efficiency and correctness.\\n\\nThis aligns with the repository's stated mission: \\\"Claude Code Marketplace Plugin with modular skills (specialized knowledge, workflows, tools).\\\" Trigger-aware validation is not language-specific; it is a meta-process control that improves the reliability of the local CI validation layer for all contributors.\\n\\n**Citation**: Repository mission in `.claude/CLAUDE.md` line 9.\\n\\n### Design Principle Alignment\\n\\nThe proposed change conforms to all established design patterns in this repository:\\n\\n1. **PEP 723 Inline Scripts**: The new `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` follows the exact pattern established by `repair_symlinks.py` and `check_symlinks.py` \u2014 PEP 723 shebang, inline dependency declaration (`# dependencies = [\\\"ruamel.yaml>=0.18.0\\\"]`), no modification to `pyproject.toml`. This is the standard pattern for root-level utility scripts.\\n   - **Citation**: `/home/user/claude_skills/scripts/repair_symlinks.py` lines 1-5, `/home/user/claude_skills/scripts/check_symlinks.py` lines 1-4\\n\\n2. **Pre-commit Hook Integration**: The script will be wired into `.pre-commit-config.yaml` as a `local` repo hook with `pass_filenames: false` (like `repair-symlinks` at line 37-42), following the established pattern for scripts that read git state directly rather than accepting filenames as arguments.\\n   - **Citation**: `/home/user/claude_skills/.pre-commit-config.yaml` lines 25-50\\n\\n3. **Dependency Management**: Use of `ruamel.yaml` for YAML parsing is consistent with the repository's explicit YAML library policy. The library is already declared in workspace dependencies and used in existing scripts (`rename_plan_files.py`).\\n   - **Citation**: `.claude/rules/yaml-toml-libraries.md`: \\\"Use `ruamel.yaml` for all YAML operations\\\"\\n\\n4. **Python Language Conventions**: Script filename uses `snake_case` (`validate_relevant_workflows.py`), consistent with repository conventions for scripts in `scripts/` directory.\\n   - **Citation**: `.claude/rules/language-conventions.md`: \\\"Use snake_case for Python file names\\\"\\n\\n### Historical Direction Alignment\\n\\nThe proposed feature directly extends the work committed in the parent item #1933 (\\\"Add local GitHub Actions schema validation to pre-commit\\\"). The trigger-aware filtering refines the scope of #1933's validation hook by computing which workflows would actually fire for a given staged change set.\\n\\nThis follows a consistent pattern in this repository: **implement a baseline feature, then add filtering/optimization on top**. Examples:\\n- `repair_symlinks.py` detects destroyed symlinks; subsequent improvements add absolute-symlink normalization\\n- The pre-commit system itself (lines 14-173 of `.pre-commit-config.yaml`) establishes a core set of hooks, then layers specialized hooks like `auto-sync-manifests` and `check-agent-auto-discovery`\\n\\nThe parent item #1933 is confirmed groomed with `status:groomed` label, and the RT-ICA snapshot in the current item confirms all prerequisite conditions are AVAILABLE (wrkflw v0.8.0 supports `--diff`, `--changed-files`, and strict-filter mode; `ruamel.yaml` is present; workflow files contain parseable `paths:` filters).\\n\\n**Citation**: Backlog item #1933 status confirmed via `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view` (accessed 2026-04-25); Fact-Check section claim 6 VERIFIED.\\n\\n### Scope and Mission Boundary\\n\\nNo expansion beyond the mission. The feature:\\n- Does NOT introduce language-specific logic (it is language-agnostic, applying to all developers)\\n- Does NOT add arbitrary human checkpoints (it is a deterministic filtering mechanism)\\n- Does NOT change the 7-stage pipeline or SAM orchestration (no relation to development-harness plugin)\\n- Does NOT require new MCP tools or artifact types\\n- Operates entirely within the pre-commit/local-validation layer, a routine maintenance domain\\n\\nThe feature is a refinement of existing process validation infrastructure, not an expansion into new territory.\\n\\n### Known Concerns\\n\\nThe impact analysis identifies three HIGH-risk areas that must be addressed in implementation:\\n\\n1. **CI Context Mismatch** (`git diff --cached` returns empty in CI): The `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` file-hygiene job (lines 171\u2013192) must add the new hook id (e.g., `validate-relevant-workflows`) to the `SKIP` list at line 185. Without this, the hook will produce incorrect SKIPPED output for all workflows or fail on strict-mode errors in CI context where no staging area exists.\\n   - **Risk**: HIGH | **Owner**: Implementation Phase | **Verification**: Hook id added to SKIP list before merge\\n\\n2. **Documentation Staleness** (`.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` Phase 4): Phase 4 currently states \\\"Validate YAML syntax: `python3 -m yaml <file>`\\\" which is stale for both #1933 (structural validation hook) and #1934 (trigger-aware filtering). Must be updated to describe both features. This file is loaded as agent instruction by every contributor executing the CI Workflow Modification Protocol.\\n   - **Risk**: HIGH | **Owner**: Documentation Phase | **Verification**: Phase 4 updated to describe both hooks and their interaction\\n\\n3. **Test Coverage** (no existing tests): The core path-matching, `on:` block parsing, and event-classification logic is non-trivial and has no automated test coverage. Acceptance criteria test (manual WOULD FIRE / SKIPPED output observation) is not sufficient for confidence in complex glob matching.\\n   - **Risk**: HIGH | **Owner**: Test Phase | **Verification**: Unit tests added for path-matching logic before merge\\n\\nThese concerns do not alter the alignment assessment because they are implementation/integration concerns, not design-intent violations. The proposed change is well-aligned with product mission and historical direction; the risks are execution risks requiring standard mitigation in integration and test phases.\\n\\n### Conclusion\\n\\nThe proposed change to add trigger-aware diff filtering is **ALIGNED** with:\\n- Repository mission (development process quality infrastructure)\\n- Design principles (PEP 723 scripts, pre-commit hook patterns, established libraries)\\n- Historical direction (optimization-on-baseline pattern from multiple prior features)\\n- Scope boundaries (no mission expansion)\\n\\nNo alignment concerns identified. The feature is a natural, well-precedented extension of the local validation layer.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **add trigger-aware diff filtering to local workflow validation** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md \u2014 pattern: Trigger-Aware Filtering and Diff Integration (README lines 128-149)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-25\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:32adbe005e27046e42266f804e03a16f -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-25)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 This is a follow-on refinement to #1933 (the base actionlint/wrkflw validation hook). It is not a blocker for the base feature but substantially improves signal quality: without trigger-aware filtering, every commit that touches workflow files runs validation on all 7 workflows regardless of which would actually fire, and every commit that does NOT touch workflow files provides no coverage signal. The wasted validation is low cost per-commit but the coverage gap (false confidence when a would-fire workflow is not validated) is the higher concern.\\n\\nLower urgency than #1933 because it requires #1933 to be shipped first. P1 is correct given the coverage gap risk \u2014 a silent false negative (skipping validation of a workflow that would fire) is worse than wasted work (validating workflows that would not fire).\\n\\nEffort: Medium-High (new Python script with non-trivial YAML parsing, glob matching, event-type classification, and PEP 723 inline-deps pattern; plus hook wiring, CI SKIP-list update, documentation update, and unit tests for the matching logic).\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: every developer running prek pre-commit who modifies non-workflow files experiences unnecessary validation of all 7 workflows on every staged commit touching workflows.\\n- Bottleneck: the coverage gap is the primary risk \u2014 a developer staging a change that would trigger `code-quality.yml` receives no validation that `code-quality.yml` is structurally valid for that trigger. The \\\"all workflows validated regardless\\\" behavior masks which workflows are actually at risk.\\n- Affects: all contributors to this repository who run prek locally and all PRs where the `file-hygiene` job runs (after #1933 ships).\\n- Secondary: agents following `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` Phase 4 (currently stale) will not invoke trigger-aware validation, defeating both #1933 and #1934 simultaneously.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Validation output becomes actionable: \\\"WOULD FIRE: code-quality.yml\\\" tells the developer exactly which workflows are at risk for their staged change.\\n- Wasted validation eliminated: staging `.claude/backlog/foo.md` will show `SKIPPED (no paths match): code-quality.yml` instead of running full validation on a workflow that would not fire.\\n- Coverage gap closed: staging `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` guarantees that the workflow is validated, because the trigger-aware filter correctly identifies it as a match.\\n- `quality-gate-audit.yml` (schedule/workflow_dispatch) is correctly classified as \\\"fires regardless of diff\\\" \u2014 developers are not given false confidence that path matching applies.\\n- CI stays green: with the hook id in the `SKIP` list of `code-quality.yml` file-hygiene job, PRs touching workflow files do not produce false SKIPPED results or strict-mode failures in CI context.\\n- Documentation updated atomically: Phase 4 of `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` accurately reflects both structural validation (#1933) and trigger-aware filtering (#1934), preventing agents from silently bypassing both features.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen the feature is present and a developer stages and commits changes:\\n\\n- Staging a change to `src/foo.py` and running prek: output shows `WOULD FIRE: code-quality.yml` (no paths filter, fires on all pushes) and `SKIPPED (no paths match): backlog-sync.yml` (paths: `.claude/backlog/**` does not match `src/foo.py`).\\n- Staging a change to `.claude/backlog/item.md` and running prek: output shows `WOULD FIRE: backlog-sync.yml` and `SKIPPED: code-quality.yml` (if code-quality.yml has no matching paths filter for that path).\\n- Staging a change to `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` itself: all workflow-scoped validators fire (the workflow file itself is the changed artifact).\\n- Running with `--all` flag: all 7 workflows are validated regardless of staged change, with no SKIPPED output.\\n- `quality-gate-audit.yml` always reports \\\"FIRES REGARDLESS (schedule/workflow_dispatch)\\\" \u2014 never SKIPPED based on paths.\\n- `git diff --cached` returns empty (CI context): the hook exits cleanly via the SKIP list without producing incorrect output.\\n- `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` Phase 4 describes trigger-aware validation and the `--all` flag \u2014 agents following the protocol use the wrapper rather than manual `python3 -m yaml` checks.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `grep 'validate-relevant-workflows' .pre-commit-config.yaml` returns at least one match confirming the new hook id is registered.\\n2. Stage `src/foo.py` (or any non-workflow, non-backlog file); run the hook manually via `uv run prek run --hook-id validate-relevant-workflows`; output contains `WOULD FIRE` for `code-quality.yml` and `SKIPPED` for `backlog-sync.yml`.\\n3. Stage `.claude/backlog/item.md`; run the hook; output contains `WOULD FIRE` for `backlog-sync.yml` and `SKIPPED` for `code-quality.yml`.\\n4. Stage `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml`; run the hook; output shows validation running for all workflow-scoped validators (no SKIPPED for any workflow that has a matching trigger for `.github/workflows/**`).\\n5. Run `uv run scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py --all`; output shows all 7 workflows validated with no SKIPPED entries.\\n6. `grep 'validate-relevant-workflows' .github/workflows/code-quality.yml` returns a match confirming the hook id appears in the SKIP list of the file-hygiene job.\\n7. CI file-hygiene job passes on a clean PR that touches `.github/workflows/*.yml` (no false SKIPPED or strict-mode error from the new hook).\\n8. `grep -A5 'Phase 4' .claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` shows text describing trigger-aware validation (not the stale `python3 -m yaml` instruction).\\n9. Unit tests exist (grep confirms) covering: (a) paths-filter match for a file inside the glob, (b) paths-filter non-match for a file outside the glob, (c) absent `paths:` key treated as \\\"no filter\\\", (d) `schedule`/`workflow_dispatch` events classified as \\\"fires regardless\\\", (e) `--all` flag bypasses filter and includes all workflows.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Research | `/home/user/claude_skills/research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md` \u2014 Trigger-Aware Filtering (lines 115\u2013131) and Strict Filter Mode sections |\\n| Backlog | #1933 \u2014 parent item (actionlint/wrkflw hook); must be implemented first |\\n| Skill | /python-engineering:pre-commit \u2014 prek/pre-commit hook configuration schema and `pass_filenames: false` + `files:` pattern |\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/scripts/repair_symlinks.py` \u2014 PEP 723 inline-script pattern reference |\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/scripts/rename_plan_files.py` \u2014 ruamel.yaml import and usage reference |\\n| Config | `/home/user/claude_skills/.pre-commit-config.yaml` \u2014 existing `local` hook entries as structural reference |\\n| CI | `/home/user/claude_skills/.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` line 185 \u2014 SKIP env var to update |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: #1933 (BLOCKING \u2014 the trigger-aware wrapper delegates to the actionlint/wrkflw hook established in #1933; #1934 cannot be wired or tested without the base hook in place)\\n- Blocks: None\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium-High \u2014 The feature requires:\\n- A new Python script (`scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py`) with non-trivial logic: YAML parsing of `on:` blocks across 7 heterogeneous workflow files, glob pattern matching for `paths:`/`paths-ignore:` filters, event-type classification (schedule/workflow_dispatch vs push/pull_request), and handling of the commented-out `paths:` edge case (absent key after YAML parse).\\n- PEP 723 inline-dependency declaration (established pattern, but must be correct).\\n- Pre-commit hook wiring in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` (additive, low effort).\\n- CI SKIP-list update in `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` (single-line change, low effort).\\n- Documentation update in `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` Phase 4 (must be accurate and complete).\\n- Unit tests for path-matching, on-block parsing, and event-classification logic (no existing test infrastructure for this; requires establishing test patterns for scripts/).\\n\\nThe glob matching and event-type classification are the highest-effort components. The strict-filter error message format (Claim 3 INCONCLUSIVE) requires a discovery step during implementation.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Status | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` | NEW \u2014 does not exist | Core script; PEP 723 inline-script pattern with inline YAML parser dependency declaration |\\n| `.pre-commit-config.yaml` | MODIFY | Add new `local` hook entry with `pass_filenames: false` and `files:` regex scoped to workflow files |\\n| `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` | MODIFY | Phase 4 must describe trigger-aware validation and `--all` flag; update atomically with #1934 completion |\\n| `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` | MODIFY | Add new hook id to `SKIP` env var in `file-hygiene` job (line 185) |\\n| `research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md` | REFERENCE ONLY | Trigger-Aware Filtering section (lines 115\u2013131) and Strict Filter Mode section \u2014 no changes needed |\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n\\n**Rationale**: The system allows unconditional workflow validation without consulting the workflow's `paths:` filter against staged changes. This creates two gaps: (1) wasted validation on workflows that would not fire for the staged change, and (2) incomplete coverage if the local validator omits relevant workflows. The fix is not code repair but a design control \u2014 trigger-aware filtering logic to map `git diff` against workflow `on:` blocks before validation.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: N/A\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Pre-commit validates all workflows regardless of staged changes, causing wasted work and incomplete coverage \u2192 Pre-commit validates only workflows whose `paths:` filters match the staged change set, preventing both waste and gaps\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Stage a change to any non-workflow file (e.g., `touch src/foo.py && git add src/foo.py`).\\n2. Run `uv run prek run --all-files` or commit to trigger the pre-commit hook pipeline.\\n3. Observe: every `.github/workflows/*.yml` file is validated, including `backlog-sync.yml` whose `paths: [\\\".claude/backlog/**\\\"]` filter would never match `src/foo.py` on push.\\n4. Observe: `quality-gate-audit.yml` (schedule/workflow_dispatch only) is also validated despite having no push/PR trigger at all.\\n5. Observe: no output distinguishes workflows that would fire from those that would not.\\n\\nAlternate path (coverage gap):\\n\\n1. Stage a change to `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` and commit.\\n2. Observe: the hook validates the changed file structurally (from #1933) but does not report whether code-quality.yml's trigger conditions would have matched the staged change set.\\n3. No \\\"WOULD FIRE\\\" / \\\"SKIPPED\\\" classification is printed.\\n\\n## Source\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nResearch entry: `./research/developer-tools/wrkflw.md`, \\\"Trigger-Aware Filtering and Diff Integration\\\" and \\\"Strict Filter Mode (v0.8.0 Breaking Change)\\\" sections.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun `git diff --cached --name-only | uv run scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py`. Output contains `WOULD FIRE: code-quality.yml` for an `src/foo.py` change and `SKIPPED (no paths match): backlog-sync.yml` for the same change. Exit code 0 