: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view` on 2026-04-25\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA new `scripts/validate_relevant_workflows.py` script that:\\n\\n1. Reads the staged file list from `git diff --cached --name-only`\\n2. Parses each `.github/workflows/*.yml` `on:` block (`paths`, `branches`, `paths-ignore`)\\n3. Reports which workflows WOULD fire for the staged change\\n4. Validates ONLY those workflows (or marks \\\"skipped \u2014 no path match\\\" for the rest)\\n\\nWired in as a pre-commit hook that runs `actionlint`/`wrkflw validate` on the relevant subset, with a `--all` flag to validate every workflow regardless of diff. Strict-mode default per wrkflw v0.8.0: if `--event` is given but no diff, exit 1 with a precise error naming the missing inputs.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThere is no current local mechanism that maps `git diff` against the `paths:` filter on a workflow's `on:` block to determine whether the workflow would actually fire for the staged change. Pre-commit hooks today either run on every staged file matching a `files:` pattern or always run \u2014 they do not consult the workflow `on:` block.\\n\\nThis means a developer modifying `src/foo.py` might break `code-quality.yml` (which fires on `paths: ['src/**']`) but the pre-commit hook validating `code-quality.yml` runs regardless of whether the staged change would even trigger that workflow on push. Conversely, when a workflow has `paths:` filters that the staged change does not match, validating it is wasted work.\\n\\nThis dependency lands on top of the parent item \\\"Add local GitHub Actions schema validation to pre-commit\\\" \u2014 once a validator is wired in, trigger-aware filtering refines its scope.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"715930c5b3103741cb867f8342c51e2ae64aa7665417fdb990c4bee31303cce6\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPiJBkg\"}"}